FPC1264 trusted application: verified stock ROM provenance

The ten hashes in SHA256.json identify fpcliu.mdt and fpcliu.b00 through
fpcliu.b08 from Xiaomi Pad 6 Pro (liuqin) HyperOS OS2.0.6.0.VMYCNXM,
Android 15, images/NON-HLOS.bin, FAT directory /image.

The standalone liuqin-fingerprint package contains the hashes and this notice,
not the OEM binaries. The default Fedora image builder extracts these ten files
from the user's SHA-pinned NON-HLOS.bin and seeds the rootfs firmware cache,
verifying the complete image and every extracted file. Original firmware bytes
and their rights remain unchanged; the project has not established a separate
grant to redistribute them under its source license.

Normal boots reuse the verified cache. If it is absent, provision_firmware.py
can mount this tablet's modem_a read-only and extract the same ten pinned files.
A missing/different stock image then disables fingerprint startup without
modifying modem_a or preventing password login.

The files are generic OEM trusted applications, not fingerprints, enrollment
credentials, device calibration or Android user data. This package creates no
biometric identity during build/install/firmware provisioning. A root-private
random credential is created only on a Polkit-authorized native enrollment.
